{"content":"🚨 **BLOCKING: the empty-hits early return drops the truncation/continuation footer — the PR's own test fails (re-verified on this head)**

`formatSessionSearchOutcome` returns `'no records match …'` before ever looking at `outcome.truncated`, so a scan that stopped early with zero hits on this page reports a flat "no records match" — with no continuation hint and no stop reason. Re-verified locally on head `66f5fc4f0`:

```text
test/tools/session_search_tool_test.dart: a capped scan carries the continuation hint (failed)
Expected: contains '"continuation": 21'
Actual:   'no records match (examined 12 of 34 records)'
```

A zero-hit page is a realistic outcome (e.g. the time budget expired before the first match), and in that case the current wording is actively misleading: it tells the model the archive has no matches when the scan in fact never finished. Fix by surfacing the truncation state before the empty-hits return:

```dart
if (outcome.hits.isEmpty) {
  final lines = <String>[
    'no records match (examined ${outcome.recordsExamined} of '
        '${outcome.recordsTotal} records)',
    if (outcome.truncated) _truncationFooter(outcome),
  ];
  return lines.join('\n');
}
```

(shared with the non-empty footer so both paths stay consistent).
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment04.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: O(n²) list building in the map fold**

`checkpointEntries = [...checkpointEntries, CheckpointMapEntry(...)]` rebuilds the whole growing list once per checkpoint. Map mode is exactly the mode agents run over giant archives, where thousands of checkpoints are plausible — n² copying of a growing list for no benefit. Use a local growable list and assign once:

```dart
final entries = <CheckpointMapEntry>[];
for (var i = 0; i < _checkpoints.length; i++) {
  ...
  entries.add(CheckpointMapEntry(...));
}
checkpointEntries = entries;
```
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment10.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: public API exposes the private `_ScanAccumulator` (and is exported from the barrel)**

`scanSessionSegment` is a public function taking the private `_ScanAccumulator` type, which `dart analyze` flags as `library_private_types_in_public_api` (verified on this head: `info - lib/src/session/session_search.dart:602:3`), and `lib/flutter_agent_harness.dart` now exports this library — so the oddity is part of the package's public surface. Either make the function private (it looks like an implementation detail of `searchSessionFile`; nothing outside the library calls it), or promote the accumulator type to public if hosts are meant to drive incremental scans themselves.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment12.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: brittle cross-tool string contract — the scheduled id is parsed out of a human-readable result line**

`recordPendingWait` recovers the scheduled-message id by regexing the `schedule_message` tool's result text (`'scheduled $id for $due — …'`, produced at `schedule_message_tool.dart:93`). This couples two modules through a display string: any wording/format change in `schedule_message_tool.dart` silently disables pending-wait arming, and per the discarded-return issue above, nothing would surface that. The new tests pin today's wording, so CI would catch it — but a structured channel is sturdier: e.g. have the schedule tool expose the id via `ToolExecutionResult` metadata, or key the ledger entry off a stable field of the arguments/session instead of the result line. At minimum, a comment on the tool's result-formatting line pointing here would keep the two sides discoverable.
