💡 **SUGGESTION: the nested-quantifier screen misses the wrapped classic shape — `((a+))+` parses**

Verified empirically on this head: `((a+))+` (the exact shape the rejection message names, wrapped one group deep) PASSES the screen, while `(a+)+`, `(a+)*`, `(a+){2,}`, `(?:\w+)+` and `([0-9]+)+` are all correctly rejected. The reason: when a group closes, its `hasQuantified` flag is stored in `lastGroupHasQuantified` but is not propagated to the PARENT group's body flag — so the outer group's body is recorded as "not quantified" even though it contains a quantified group, and the outer `+` then slips through. `(((a+)))+` and friends evade the same way.

This lands in the documented residual ("one unscreened pathological match on a capped record can still exceed the budget"), so it does not block — but the fix is one line: when popping a group that carries a quantified body, also OR the flag into the new stack top (a group whose body contains a quantified group is itself quantified-risk):

```dart
case ')':
  if (groupHasQuantified.isNotEmpty) {
    final popped = groupHasQuantified.removeLast();
    lastGroupHasQuantified = popped;
    if (groupHasQuantified.isNotEmpty && popped) {
      groupHasQuantified.last = true;
    }
    lastAtomWasGroup = true;
    lastAtomQuantified = false;
  }
```

A test pinning `((a+))+` as rejected would keep the corner covered.

